{"title":"The Ember-Wind and the Quiet River","sections":["In a valley where mountains held their breath, there was a river that spoke only in whispers. The villagers believed the water carried memories of the first spark, when the world learned to dream. At dusk, a young girl named Lira wandered along the bank, listening to the river’s murmur of heat and ice, of rivers that forgot their names and rivers that remembered their mothers’ lullabies. One evening, a coal-black ember rolled from a distant furnace of the mountains and settled in the river’s current. It burned with a patient light, neither bright nor dark, just a constant promise. When the ember touched the water, the river trembled, and the whispers grew into a song that asked for a story to warm its core.

Lira, who carried no fire within her, asked the ember for a tale in return for a chance to dream herself. The ember spoke of a wind that kept a map of every star and a river that kept the footprints of every traveler. It spoke of a time when courage was a spark, and a spark could become a village, a village a season, and a season a memory.

So Lira lit a single flame in her palm, not to burn, but to guide. The ember glowed brighter, the river sang louder, and the valley learned to dream again, not by force or fury, but by listening. The quiet river became a doorway to every possible dawn."]}

